00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.340 align:start position:0%
about 30,000 stakeholders representing  the interests of corporations NGOs think  tanks public authorities trade business  or professional associations are based  in Brussels some of them walk in and out  of the EU institutions every day they're  able to shape policies that match the  needs and realities of citizens  businesses and other organizations in  the Member States it's a practice that  dates back to the 1830s when groups  representing private interests used to  roam the lobbies in the House of Commons  in London to put their case to MPs to  answer European citizens concerns about  the independence of EU institutions a  European transparency register was  created in 2011 this is an online public  database where organizations have to  register and are invited to declare who  they are who they represent what they  want and how much they've spent on  lobbying entries must be accurate  complete and up-to-date and all those on  the register must sign a code of conduct  every new organisation asking for  registration will be checked and can be  removed if there's a complaint  I hope 2016 over 9,000 stakeholders  registered in return they can access the  EU institutions premises to meet civil  servants commissioners and MEP s and sit  on expert panels transparency register  ensures greater scrutiny of lobbying  covering both the lobbyist and of the  civil servants and anybody's who meet  them but lobbyists do not comply with  the specified code of conduct they lose  their access to the EU authorities the  European Parliament and European  Commission use the transparency register  the console acts merely as an observer  finally in continuing efforts to  increase transparency and openness in  the EU policymaking process the  Parliament is calling on the Commission  to make the registration of stakeholders  mandatory by 2017
 

